  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the P in PID do?",
      "a": "P stands for proportional. It pushes harder the bigger the error is right now, like steering more sharply the further you have drifted from the middle of the lane.",
      "asks": [
        "What is proportional control?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the I in PID do?",
      "a": "I stands for integral. It adds up the error over time, so even a small error that will not go away builds a bigger and bigger correction until it is gone.",
      "asks": [
        "What is integral control?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the D in PID do?",
      "a": "D stands for derivative. It reacts to how fast the error is changing, easing off as the target gets close so the machine does not overshoot, a bit like braking gently before a stop sign.",
      "asks": [
        "What is derivative control?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the error in PID?",
      "a": "The error is the target minus the measured value. If a drone should be level but is tilted 5 degrees, the error is 5 degrees, and the controller works to make it zero.",
      "asks": [
        "How is the error calculated?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is PID tuning?",
      "a": "Tuning means choosing how strong each of the P, I and D parts should be. Too strong and the machine wobbles or overshoots; too weak and it is slow to reach the target.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does my drone wobble?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "PID controllers are the most widely used feedback controllers in industry.",
    "Nicolas Minorsky published an analysis of PID-style control for automatic ship steering in 1922."
  ],
